|term-group[scope][edge][containment][root-scope]
  :status proposed

  A **SCOPE** makes @{binding}'s informal "jurisdiction" precise: it is the unit within which @{bindings} are kept and collisions counted. @{scopes} connect to one another by labeled **EDGES**; the universal edge label is **CONTAINMENT** — pointing from a @{scope} to the @{scope} enclosing it — and a @{scope} with no outward containment edge is a **ROOT-SCOPE**. This arrangement is the economics the whole theory rides on: a @{binding} need only be unambiguous *locally*, within its @{scope}, and locally-cheap @{bindings} compose across @{edges} into global reach. No @{maintainer} ever needs a globally unique @{name}.

  |term[scope] The unit of resolution locality: the jurisdiction within which a maintainer's bindings are kept and collisions counted.
    :synonyms [jurisdiction namespace]
    :avoid [context region] ; both too loose to carry the locality claim
    |rels :holds     @{binding} {0,N}
          :enclosed-by @{scope} {0,1}  ; via a containment edge; absent exactly for a root-scope
          :edges     @{edge}    {0,N}
    |invariants
      - Collision is counted within one scope; two mints of one name in different scopes are simply two bindings

  |term[edge] A labeled, directed connection from one scope to another, traversable when resolving.
    |rels :from  @{scope} {1,1}
          :to    @{scope} {1,1}
          :label @{name}  {1,1}
    |invariants
      - Labels beyond containment are an open extension point; what an edge's label means is priced by resolution policy, not fixed here

  |term[containment] The universal edge label: the edge from a scope to the scope enclosing it.

  |term[root-scope] A scope with no containment edge outward.
    |invariants
      - Every scope reaches at least one root-scope through containment

  |discussion
    - Every line of the mailing address names a scope; "Joseph" is cheap precisely because its scope is a household, and scope-composition is what makes the cheap name globally reachable.
    - Corresponds to the scope-graphs formalism (scopes, labeled edges, scoped data — ref/vanantwerpen-2018-scopes/ Fig. 1), generalized past programs; each inheritance is verified where used, not here.
  |examples
    - A household, a directory, a document's tree, a module, a namespace, the DNS root
    - Edge labels in the wild: lexical parent, module import, filesystem mount, "extends", a symlink's jump
  |working-notes
    - Deliberately left open: whether root-scopes deserve the name @{WORLD} and any one-root-per-scope claim; whether edge labels want a taxonomy or stay an open set priced per policy (lean: open set).
